// ==========================================
// 1. WHY WE USE 'super' IN METHOD OVERRIDING
// (As per notes: Overriding happens ONLY in sub class)
// ==========================================
/*
 * THE ISSUE: When a child class uses the exact same method name as the parent,
 * calling that method directly inside the child class causes an infinite loop (it keeps calling itself).
 *
 * THE SOLUTION: We use 'super.methodName()' to explicitly tell Java:
 * "Go to the parent class and run its version of the method first, then come back and run the child's extra code."
 */


// ==========================================
// 2. WHY WE USE METHOD OVERLOADING
// (As per notes: Overloading happens within the same class OR within the sub class)
// ==========================================
/*
Laptop IS-A product Mobile IS-A product
 * THE REASON: It makes the code much cleaner and easier to remember.
 * Instead of creating 100 different method names for 100 different objects
 * (like displayLaptop(), displayMobile(), displayWatch()),
 * we just use ONE single method name (like displayDetails()).
 * Java is smart enough to automatically pick the right method based on the input we pass to it.
 */